WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
Sweat cools you only when it evaporates, and the air sets how much can.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.940
That sweat is water the body chose to spend.

3
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.060
Why doesn't the rest seep out through the skin?

4
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.740
The outer surface is made of dead cells.

5
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.380
So what holds the water in?

6
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.930
The outer layer, the epidermis, has no blood vessels.

7
00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.130
Its cells divide at the base and move outward, like a conveyor belt.

8
00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:32.310
On the way they fill with keratin and die.

9
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:38.130
They end as corneocytes: flat cells packed with keratin, with no nucleus.

10
00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.580
This wall keeps most water in, so the body can spend water on purpose.

11
00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.200
A small, steady leak crosses all the time.

12
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.100
Large losses go through sweat ducts, when the brain calls for them.

13
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:54.243
Where is the seal?

14
00:00:55.863 --> 00:01:00.043
Gut and kidney linings seal the spaces between their cells with tight junctions.

15
00:01:01.463 --> 00:01:05.403
So the natural guess was that skin does too, between its living cells.

16
00:01:06.517 --> 00:01:12.897
In 1975, Peter Elias and Daniel Friend tested that guess in newborn mouse skin.

17
00:01:14.197 --> 00:01:18.757
They followed tracers that dissolve in water and look dense under the electron

18
00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:23.277
microscope. The tracers travel in the spaces between cells.

19
00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:28.057
If tight junctions were the seal, the tracers would stop there.

20
00:01:29.017 --> 00:01:31.997
Instead, the tracers slipped past the lower layers.

21
00:01:33.297 --> 00:01:35.317
They stopped high in the granular layer.

22
00:01:36.677 --> 00:01:41.197
There, lipid-filled granules called lamellar bodies had emptied into the spaces

23
00:01:41.197 --> 00:01:45.657
between cells. The authors placed the main barrier in that lipid.

24
00:01:46.717 --> 00:01:52.197
Later work showed this lipid as patterned layers between corneocytes, often pictured

25
00:01:52.197 --> 00:01:53.297
as bricks in mortar.

26
00:01:54.737 --> 00:02:01.277
Here is the idea. Skin's main water seal is lipid packed between dead cells, not

27
00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:02.797
junctions between living ones.

28
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.900
Then tight junctions came back.

29
00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:10.600
Mice were bred without claudin-1, a tight junction protein.

30
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.640
If lipid is the main seal, should losing that protein matter much?

31
00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.120
It mattered a great deal.

32
00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:29.140
In 2002, Furuse and colleagues found these mice died within a day of birth.

33
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.280
They lost too much water through the skin.

34
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.920
So tight junctions in the granular layer also contribute.

35
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.733
So the seal works in layers.

36
00:02:41.153 --> 00:02:45.933
Dead cells, mortared with lipid and helped by junctions beneath, hold your water in.

37
00:02:47.113 --> 00:02:51.673
The body then spends water where it chooses, through sweat ducts, and the air

38
00:02:51.673 --> 00:02:53.493
decides how much can evaporate.
